 ],
 "literal": "Other than the way He who apportioned has apportioned, to those who have gathered ten million, enjoying is difficult.",
 "translation": "Except in the way ordained by the Apportioner, it is difficult even for those who have amassed millions to enjoy their wealth.",
 "modern": "வகுத்தவன் வகுத்துக் கொடுத்த வழியிலல்லாமல், கோடிக்கணக்கான பொருள்களைச் சேர்த்து வைத்தவர்களுக்கும் அவற்றை அனுபவிப்பது கடினமாகும்.",
 "explanation": "This couplet highlights the supreme power of destiny over human possession and effort. The word 'வகுத்தான்' refers to the Apportioner, the force that pre-ordains each individual's share of experiences. Even if a person succeeds in accumulating immense wealth, represented here by 'கோடி' (ten million), they cannot enjoy ('துய்த்தல்') it unless it is in the manner ('வகை') allotted to them. Thus, the poet emphasizes that possession does not guarantee enjoyment, as our experiences are ultimately governed by destiny.",
